# syscall-cap.manifest - the syscall-cap-lint classification (Stage 1 of the
# system capability API, docs/SYSTEM_CAPABILITY_API.md).
#
# tools/syscall-cap-lint/syscall-cap-lint auto-discovers every SYS_* with a
# dispatcher case in proc/syscall.c. Each MUST be classified here as EITHER
# [NOCAP] (a conscious "requires no capability") OR [CAP] with the capability
# class it is gated by. A dispatched syscall in neither FAILS THE BUILD: a new
# syscall defaults to UNGATED and builds clean, and this lint makes that default
# a build failure so an ambient capability cannot be added by omission. Wired as
# an order-only prerequisite of kernel.elf.
#
# [CAP] lines are cross-checked against rustkern/caps.rs cap_required_for_syscall
# (through proc/syscall.h numbers): the class here MUST equal the class the
# kernel actually gates with, in both directions. The manifest cannot describe a
# gate the kernel does not enforce, and a kernel gate that is not declared here
# fails the build.
#
# The [NOCAP] set below was bulk-baselined at Stage 1 from the dispatcher (like
# the concurrency-lint baseline): it is NOT a per-syscall review that each of
# these SHOULD be ungated, it is a record that each is CURRENTLY ungated. As
# capabilities are wired in Stage 2+ (input.inject, audio.output, net.connect,
# serial.port), move the syscall from [NOCAP] to [CAP] in the same change that
# adds its gate to caps.rs. First token per line is the SYS_* name.
#
[NOCAP]
SYS_BLK_ENUM
SYS_BLK_READ
SYS_BLK_WRITE
SYS_PART_PREPARE
SYS_PART_WRITE
SYS_MKFS
SYS_MOUNT_LIST
SYS_MOUNT
